mod:pointer_target_also_holder;
// 2026-11-12 (landing 177d2b5, row 5: DEF-661, and row 4's nine releases). A HOLDER THAT IS ITSELF A TARGET OF A POINTER IS STILL READ FOR WHAT IT HOLDS.
// In `HL->:hp = raw get(@q);` with `get` = `pass (<-(qq.sp)).p;`, `q` holding `@s` and `s` holding `@h`, `hp` points at `h`. The aliasing analysis reads what `hp`
// points at off the chain: `q`'s address, then what `q` holds (`@s`), then what `s` holds (`@h`). Before this row the walk ended at a holder that was itself a
// target of the pointer: where `S` could hold an `HL` (a field, an element, a payload, anything behind a `List` or a slice) it took `s` as the target and never
// read what `s` holds. So `h` was no target, a growth through `hp` wrote to no one, and an address into `h.l` held across the growth read freed heap. Now a holder
// that is a target is a target AND is read.
// One cell a face, its names suffixed with the cell's. Each face was accepted by row 4's commit (a9d0aa7) and read freed memory: 236 at -O0 and -O2 on roads 0 to
// 2, and 97 (MachineFault) under the freed-heap detector. BORROW-015 each now.
// ROW 4'S NINE RELEASES (the gate attack's cells, Ailp-team's; rulings "The gate attack's release road is ruled"). `S` holds a slice of the pointee type, and row 4
// made a slice a holder of its element type (the holds test's slice arm), so `s` became a target and its pointer field went unread. Each was refused on main
// (169d6d4) and on row 3's commit (a633585), and released by row 4 --
//   `z_sl` (the frame above, `HL[]:sl` beside the pointer field; the growth in a callee), `z_slw` (the growth in the frame, `list_push(@hp.l, …)`);
//   `z_sl2` (a slice of a struct that holds the type), `z_ss` (a slice of slices);
//   `z_sl3` (three holders deep, the getter reading two pointers), `z_sl3m` (the same with the slice in the outermost holder), `z_slpl` (the holder's address kept
//   in a pointer local first);
//   `z_slv`, `z_slvp` (the other side: the address taken through `hp`, the growth by `h`'s own name, in a callee and in the frame).
// DEF-661'S TWO (the gate attack's): the same stop where the holder was a target before row 4, so they were accepted on main too -- `z_ar` (an array of the type,
// `HL[1]:sl`), `z_lsf` (a `List` of it).
// THE ROAD WIDENED (nitpick-compiler_116's cells; rulings "Row 5's prices and its two faces to read are ruled"). Accepted on main (169d6d4), on row 3's commit
// (a633585) and on b510126 (177d2b3) too, by what makes the holder a target --
//   `w_bv` (a by-value field of the type), `w_opt` (an optional of it), `w_nest` (a struct that holds it), `w_gen` (a generic struct's instance at it);
//   `w_node` (the holder IS the pointee type: a node whose own pointer field points at the next node);
//   `w_any` (the pointer is an `any->`, which every address on its chain is a target of);
//   the two sides and the growth's two places, on those holders: `w_arw`, `w_lsw`, `w_bvw` (the growth through `hp` in the frame), `w_arv`, `w_lsv`, `w_bvv`,
//   `w_nodev` (the address through `hp`, the growth by name in a callee), `w_arvp` (the growth by name in the frame).
// `w_xc` is a release on main, made by landing 177d2b4's row 7 (DEF-585's retyped pointer; b510126 refuses it): the holder holds no `HL`, and its address is
// retyped through `any->` on its road, which makes it a target of every pointee.
// And row 4's releases beyond the nine, each refused on main -- `w_asl` (an array of slices), `w_par` (the stored pointer is a pointer parameter's value, the held
// address through the parameter), `w_parh` (the holder is a by-value parameter the body stores into; b510126 accepted it and landing 177d2b4 had closed it),
// `w_mem` (the holder's address read out of a member into a pointer local first), `w_getl` (the getter reads the holder's address into a local of its own first).
// RIGHT PROGRAMS THE ROW REFUSES (DEF-662, LOW, OPEN; the same ruling). A POINTER INTO A TARGET HOLDER'S OWN MEMBER IS TAKEN AS REACHING WHAT THE HOLDER'S POINTER
// MEMBERS POINT AT. The getter hands back `@((<-(qq.sp)).own)`, an address inside `s` itself, and the held address is into `h`, which `s.p` points at. The growth
// lands on `s.own` and can't move `h.l`: each ran 67. A target is a root to this walk, so `s`'s own members and the storage its pointer members reach are one
// holder. Each is refused at the growth, against the held address, never with a wrong run --
//   the two-hop forms main accepted, which this row refuses: `p_own_ok` (a by-value member), `p_ar_ok` (an array member), `p_ls_ok` (a `List` member), and their
//   twins with the address taken in the caller as the getter takes it (`hp = @((<-(q.sp)).own)`), `p_owni_ok`, `p_ari_ok`;
//   `p_sl_ok` (a slice member): refused on main and on row 3's commit, accepted by row 4, refused again -- main's answer restored;
//   THE ONE-HOP TWINS, refused on every build, b510126 included, each at its two-hop form's code and site: `p_ownd_ok`, `p_ard_ok`, `p_lsd_ok`, `p_sld_ok` (the
//   address off the holder's own name, `hp = @s.own`), `p_own1_ok`, `p_ar1_ok`, `p_ls1_ok`, `p_sl1_ok` (a getter over `@s`). The row gives the two-hop form the
//   answer the one-hop form always had: that parity is what these eight pin;
//   `p_lsi_ok`, `p_sli_ok` (the caller-side twins over a `List` and a slice member): refused from main on.
// The prices move to the _ok file when DEF-662's row lands: at a target, an address of one of its own members is that member, and only a pointer member read out
// is followed.
// STILL OPEN, and in no cell here (the gate attack's cells, each accepted with this row too): a getter handed a local that holds the parameter's pointer
// (DEF-657: `z_slm`, `z_slm_ns`); a callee handed `@q` that grows through the stored pointer (DEF-610: `z_slcal`); one call handed two ways to one owner,
// `w2(q, @h)` (DEF-631: `z_slbv`, with DEF-596's by-value take beside it). And a `ralloc` or `dalloc` through a header's `.ptr` (DEF-660, `wild` only).
error:E5;
struct:K_z_sl = { int64:k; };
func:mk_z_sl = List<int64>() never fails { List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(2i64); drop list_push(@l, 1i64); drop list_push(@l, 2i64); pass l; };
func:growl_z_sl = int64(List<int64>->:l) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
struct:HL_z_sl = { List<int64>:l; int64:k; };
struct:S_z_sl = { HL_z_sl[]:sl; HL_z_sl->:p; };
struct:Q_z_sl = { S_z_sl->:sp; int64:z; };
func:mkh_z_sl = HL_z_sl() never fails { pass HL_z_sl{ l: raw mk_z_sl(), k: 1i64 }; };
func:growh_z_sl = int64(HL_z_sl->:w) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(@w.l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:get_z_sl = HL_z_sl->(Q_z_sl->:qq) never fails { pass (<-(qq.sp)).p; };
func:g_z_sl = int64(int64:n) never fails {
    HL_z_sl:h = raw mkh_z_sl();
    HL_z_sl[1]:arr = [raw mkh_z_sl()];
    S_z_sl:s = S_z_sl{ sl: arr[0i64...1i64], p: @h };
    Q_z_sl:q = Q_z_sl{ sp: @s, z: 1i64 };
    HL_z_sl->:hp = raw get_z_sl(@q);
    int64->:e = @h.l[0i64];
    discard(raw growh_z_sl(hp));
    pass (<-e) + 6i64;
};
func:f_z_sl = int64(int64:n) never fails { pass raw g_z_sl(n); };
func:cell_main_z_sl = int32() never fails { pass (((raw f_z_sl(1i64)) + 60i64) =>! int32); };
